[BLAME] benchmark_2/parts/semver_solver/pretense/semver_solver_impl_pretense.py: [slug:semver.solver.compatible_assignment] [viol:pret_cov.transition_frequency_starvation] Facet: compatible forward checking dependency on assigned package Witness: graph='a 1.0.0 (no deps), b 1.0.0 depends on a>=1.0.0; roots a==1.0.0, b==1.0.0'. The resolve contract must accept a dependency range against an already assigned package when the assigned version satisfies that range and then continue the search to a complete mutually compatible assignment. The only forward-checking scenario currently exercised drives the incompatible case, so the accepting side of that check is never taken: no deterministic scenario assigns the dependency target first and then selects a dependent whose declared range on it is satisfied, even though the search proceeds past that check to a successful outcome. A fixed graph with the target selected before the dependent, for example target a==1.0.0 and dependent b==1.0.0 requiring a>=1.0.0, is valid under the specification and reaches the accepting transition.
[CHANGE] benchmark_2/parts/semver_solver/pretense/semver_solver_impl_pretense.py: Address fifth coverage round: add the accepting side of forward checking as a deterministic scenario. Target a 1.0.0 has no dependencies and dependent b 1.0.0 requires a>=1.0.0; roots a==1.0.0 and b==1.0.0 assign a first (MRV plus lexical tie-break), then b is selected, its declared range on the already assigned a is merged and satisfies the assignment, and the search continues to a complete mutually compatible outcome selecting a 1.0.0 and b 1.0.0.
